    <Paragraph GutterBottom="false">
        Nothing fancy happening here. One important thing to note is that
        <Highlight>MLIR expects every operation
            emitted to have a location associated with it</Highlight>. The location information is the source file,
        line, and column &ndash; standard stuff you might expect for a debugger to match assembly with the source code
        or the compiler to use when reporting errors.
    </Paragraph>

    <Header2>Parsing Using lalrpop</Header2>

    <Paragraph GutterBottom="false">
        Using lalrpop is not unlike using similar tools, e.g. yacc or Bison. We define the language grammar in a special
        <Code>.lalrpop</Code> file and lalrpop generates a lexer<Footnote>
            Lexer, scanner, tokeniser, however you want to call it. I'll consistently use &ldquo;lexer&rdquo;.
        </Footnote> and a parser. However, the aforementioned location information is why we cannot just use lalrpop's lexer
        directly &ndash; it only gathers the byte offset within the file with no regard for line/column info.<Footnote>
            This is not a fundamental problem and there is
            <LinkA Href="https://github.com/lalrpop/lalrpop/issues/666">an issue</LinkA> open for lexing with
            more useful source position.
        </Footnote> Fortunately, lalrpop allows us to define a custom lexer (which is simple to code) and generate a parser
        using it (which is considerably more complicated.<Footnote>The autogenerated LALR(1) parser at the end of this
            chapter has $\sim 10\,000$ lines.</Footnote>)
    </Paragraph>

    <Header3>Lexer</Header3>

    <Paragraph>
        We scan for all meaningful special characters: <Code>* + &lt; &gt; = , ; ( ) { } [ ]</Code>; the keywords
        <Code Language="plain">return var def print</Code>; and string identifiers and numerical values.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        Implementing this in Rust is quite easy. We split the source into lines using
        <LinkA Href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/primitive.str.html#method.lines"><Code>str::lines</Code></LinkA> and then
        the current line into individual characters using
        <LinkA Href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/primitive.str.html#method.chars"><Code>str::chars</Code></LinkA>.
        This properly handles Unicode, although note that a single Unicode character is not necessarily what a user
        might consider a &ldquo;column&rdquo; visually. After that we just scan character-by-character. Identifiers
        are matched as anything that contains ASCII alphanumerical characters and underscores, and starts with a letter.
        Numbers are given to <Code>f64::from_str</Code> and invalid values are reported as lexer errors.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph GutterBottom="false">
        In the end, we map the lexer tokens into lalrpop using an <Code Language="rust">extern</Code> block in the
        <Code>.lalrpop</Code> file. Now we can write our parser rules using normal strings and the special
        <Code Language="rust">"Ident"</Code> and <Code Language="rust">"Num"</Code> tokens that map to the
        <Code Language="rust">Token::Ident(String)</Code> and <Code Language="rust">Token::Number(f64)</Code> tokens.
    </Paragraph>

    <Header3>Parser</Header3>

    <Paragraph>
        Writing a lalrpop parser is a joy. I'm not going to turn this into a lalrpop tutorial, please take a look
        at their book for details,<Footnote IsRef="true">
            <LinkA Href="https://lalrpop.github.io/lalrpop/index.html">LALRPOP: Book</LinkA>
        </Footnote> but in short
        we can define rules like:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" LanguageString="lalrpop" FileName="src/parser/syntax.lalrpop" LineNumbers="true" LineNumbersStartAt="30" Code=@(@"
pub Mod: ast::ModuleAst = {
    <Func+> => ast::ModuleAst::new(<>.into_iter().flatten().collect(), Location::new(file.clone(), 1, 1)),
}
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        This defines a public struct <Code>ModParser</Code> in the generated code that parses a string into
        <Code>ast::ModuleAst</Code>. There is only one rule, one or more matches of the <Code>Func</Code> rule (defined
        below). We define how to process matches directly with Rust code that gets emitted in the appropriate parser
        action. The objects created from that rule are passed here as a <Code>Vec</Code> (since we used <Code>+</Code> to
        match a sequence) and can be accessed via the
        <Code>&lt;&gt;</Code> token. We pass it to the <Code>ModuleAst</Code> constructor alongside a dummy location
        pointing to the start of the file. Here's the <Code>Func</Code> rule:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" LanguageString="lalrpop" FileName="src/parser/syntax.lalrpop" LineNumbers="true" LineNumbersStartAt="34" Code=@(@"
pub Func: Option<ast::FunctionAst> = {
    <p:Proto> ""{"" <e:(<TopExpr> "";"")*> ""}"" => Some(ast::FunctionAst::new(
        p, e.into_iter().flatten().collect())),
    ! => { errors.push(<>.error); None },
}
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        We parse the function prototype and then a sequence of <Code>TopExpr</Code> separated by semicolons.
        Importantly, our <Code>Func</Code> rule can fail &ndash; it returns an <Code>Option</Code>.
        There is a global vector of errors accessible to the parser, so when parsing a function fails we don't
        immediately stop &ndash; we store the error and then tell lalrpop to try and recover
        to a place where parsing can continue. This is more in line of what you expect from an actual language frontend,
        where you get all of your red squiggles immediately and not only the first one. The <Code>None</Code> values
        are simply ignored &ndash; at the top when using the parser we will not return the partially parsed AST
        if the error vector is not empty.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        The <Code>!</Code> token matches only on an error. We push the error and return <Code>None</Code> as the function.
        The function prototype is a good level to provide error recovery, as a single malformed function should not inhibit
        the parser from processing subsequent functions. You might have noticed that <Code>TopExpr</Code> also can fail:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" LanguageString="lalrpop" FileName="src/parser/syntax.lalrpop" LineNumbers="true" LineNumbersStartAt="44" Code=@(@"
TopExpr: Option<ast::TopLevelExprAst> = {
    <VarDecl>,
    <Return>,
    <Print>,
    <Expr> => <>.map(|e| ast::TopLevelExprAst::Expr(e)),
    ! => { errors.push(<>.error); None },
};
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        This is also a sensible error recovery level &ndash; if we fail to parse a top-level expression we can find the next
        token and continue parsing the function body from there.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        The rules for <Code>VarDecl</Code>, <Code>Return</Code>, and <Code>Print</Code> are straightforward, and can only
        fail if the nested expression fails. The <Code>Expr</Code> rule is interesting as it uses precedence:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" LanguageString="lalrpop" FileName="src/parser/syntax.lalrpop" LineNumbers="true" LineNumbersStartAt="70" Code=@(@"
Expr: Option<ast::ExprAst> = {
    #[precedence(level=""0"")]
    <Primary>,
    #[precedence(level=""1"")] #[assoc(side=""left"")]
    <l:Expr> <op:Loc<""*"">> <r:Expr> => l.and_then(|l| r.map(|r| {
        ast::ExprAst::BinOp(ast::BinOpData::new(ast::Op::Multiply, l.into(), r.into(), op.1))
    })),
    #[precedence(level=""2"")] #[assoc(side=""left"")]
    <l:Expr> <op:Loc<""+"">> <r:Expr> => l.and_then(|l| r.map(|r| {
        ast::ExprAst::BinOp(ast::BinOpData::new(ast::Op::Add, l.into(), r.into(), op.1))
    })),
};
Primary: Option<ast::ExprAst> = {
    #[precedence(level=""0"")]
    <Call>,
    ""("" <Expr> "")"",
    <Num> => Some(<>),
    <BracketedLiteral>,
    #[precedence(level=""1"")]
    <Var> => Some(<>),
};
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        Here we tackle the standard problem when parsing nested expressions, giving highest precedence to atomic or
        parenthesised terms, and then parsing arithmetic operators according to regular arithmetic precedence. We also have
        to define the associativity: here operators are left-associative, which means we parse e.g. $2 + 2 + 2$ as
        $(2 + 2) + 2$ and <em>not</em> as $2 + (2 + 2)$. For our operators it makes no difference, but there would be
        a difference if we had, for example, subtraction.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        A small nuance there is that we need <Code>Call</Code> to have precedence over <Code>Var</Code>, because otherwise
        there would be ambiguity on whether <Code>Ident ( Num )</Code> means &ldquo;a call to Ident with a single parameter
        Num&rdquo; or &ldquo;reference to variable Ident and then a bracketed Num expression&rdquo;.
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph>
        There are two rules that require quite complex processing: <Code>BracketedLiteral</Code> and <Code>VarType</Code>.
        First of all, a <Code>Literal</Code> has to be a tensor, so we cannot match singular values (those are <Code>Num</Code>).
        Second, literal shapes have to be consistent, for example <Code>[[1], [2, 3]]</Code> is not a valid tensor literal.
        We handle the first issue by separating our rules into the top-level <Code>BracketedLiteral</Code> that delegates
        to <Code>NestedLiteral</Code>:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" LanguageString="lalrpop" FileName="src/parser/syntax.lalrpop" Code=@(@"
BracketedLiteral: Option<ast::ExprAst> = {
    <l:Loc<""["">> <values:NestedLiteral> ""]"" => // ... snip ...
};
NestedLiteral: Vec<ast::ExprAst> = {
    CommaP<BracketedLiteral> => <>.into_iter().flatten().collect(),
    CommaP<Num>
};
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        <Code>CommaP</Code> is a macro that I defined matching a comma-separated, non-empty list of elements. <Code>Loc</Code>
        is also a macro that uses the lexer to return a pair of matched element and location. Now we implement the logic
        to figure out the dimensions of the tensor and report an error if they are inconsistent:
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" LanguageString="lalrpop" FileName="src/parser/syntax.lalrpop" LineNumbers="true" LineNumbersStartAt="96" Code=@(@"
BracketedLiteral: Option<ast::ExprAst> = {
    <l:Loc<""["">> <values:NestedLiteral> ""]"" => {
        let mut dims = vec![];
        dims.push(u64::try_from(values.len()).unwrap());
        // Gather dimensions of all nested tensor literals.
        let mut nested_dims = values.iter().filter_map(|e| match e {
            ast::ExprAst::Literal(l) => Some(l.dims()),
            _ => None,
        });
        // Assert all dimensions are equal.
        // try_reduce would be best here but it's unstable.
        if let Some(s) = nested_dims.next() {
            let nested_dim = s;
            for dim in nested_dims {
                if dim != nested_dim {
                    let err = ParseError::User {
                      error: SyntaxError::inconsistent_tensor_shape(dim, nested_dim, &l.1)
                    };
                    errors.push(err);
                    return None;
                }
            }
            dims.extend(nested_dim);
        }

        Some(ast::ExprAst::Literal(ast::LiteralData::new(values, dims, l.1)))
    }
};
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph GutterBottom="false">
        Finally finally, the <Code>VarType</Code> rule needs to assert that the dimensions inside are valid.
        Since our lexer happily eats up any numerical values, a declaration like <Code>def x&lt;3.14, -0.1&gt;</Code> may get
        to the parser. Here we make sure the value is actually a valid dimension, i.e. we need to convert <Code>f64</Code>
        to <Code>u64</Code> by first checking that the number has no fractional part, is not negative, and is not larger
        than <Code>u64::MAX</Code>.
    </Paragraph>
    <Header3>Putting It All Together</Header3>
    <Paragraph>
        We need to tell lalrpop to generate the parser on build time and then include the autogenerated module
        in our crate hierarchy. I also needed to put a bunch of clippy silencers on the mod declaration because
        the autogenerated code does not exactly follow best style practices.
    </Paragraph>
            <CodeBlock Language="rust" FileName="build.rs" LineNumbers="true" Code=@(@"
//! Setup the code generation for the crate:
//!   - lalrpop parser generation from `.lalrpop`.
fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    setup_lalrpop()?;
    Ok(())
}

fn setup_lalrpop() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    println!(""cargo::rerun-if-changed=src/parser/syntax.lalrpop"");
    lalrpop::process_src()
}
")></CodeBlock>
            <CodeBlock Language="rust" FileName="src/parser.rs" LineNumbers="true" Code=@(@"
//! Definition of the [`parse`] function that turns a .toy source file into an [`ast::ModuleAst`].
//!
//! The implementation is delegated to the parser generated by [`lalrpop`](`lalrpop_util`).
use crate::parser::{
    error::{SyntaxError, SyntaxErrors},
    lexer::Lexer,
};
use crate::{Location, SourceLocation};
use lalrpop_util::lalrpop_mod;
use std::sync::Arc;

// Generate the lalrpop module as `pub mod syntax`
// from the file generated at build time from `syntax.lalrpop`.
lalrpop_mod!(
    #[allow(missing_docs, unreachable_pub)]
    #[allow(clippy::ptr_arg, clippy::cloned_instead_of_copied, clippy::use_self, clippy::must_use_candidate, clippy::match_same_arms, clippy::if_then_some_else_none)]
    #[rustfmt::skip]
    pub syntax,
    ""/parser/syntax.rs"");
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        We can now use the <Code>ModParser</Code> to get the AST.
    </Paragraph>
    <CodeBlock Language="rust" FileName="src/parser.rs" LineNumbers="true" LineNumbersStartAt="27" Code=@(@"
/// Parse the given module from `source` code into [`ast::ModuleAst`].
///
/// The `filename` is used in error messages alongside line and column info of the source code.
///
/// # Errors
/// If any errors occur during parsing, returns them wrapped in [`SyntaxErrors`] as the [`Err`]
/// value.
pub fn parse(source: &str, filename: &Arc<String>) -> Result<ast::ModuleAst, SyntaxErrors<SyntaxError>> {
    let lexer = Lexer::new(source, filename.clone());
    let mut errors = vec![];
    // Lalrpop `parse` returns an `Err` only if it could not recover and continue parsing,
    // which shouldn't happen with the error recovery we defined in the grammar.
    // The usual path is that errors are gracefully handled and put into the `errors` vec.
    // If any errors were reported, we convert them into `SyntaxErrors` and return `Err`.
    let m = syntax::ModParser::new()
        .parse(filename, &mut errors, lexer)
        .map_err(|e| SyntaxErrors::from(vec![SyntaxError::from_lalrpop(e, filename)]))?;
    if errors.is_empty() {
        Ok(m)
    } else {
        Err(SyntaxErrors::from(
            errors
                .into_iter()
                .map(|e| SyntaxError::from_lalrpop(e, filename))
                .collect::<Vec<SyntaxError>>(),
        ))
    }
}
")></CodeBlock>
    <Paragraph>
        I wrapped it into a simple CLI binary, <Code>toyp</Code>, that takes the input from a file or standard input
        and dumps the AST. I've already shown you output for a correct file, so let's showcase the error reporting here.
    </Paragraph>
